An inspectable feed-axis motion and energy account (@mech-axis-example: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/FUNdaMENTALS-of-Design-Slocum.lingua#r_58Q6VQJ3FXXGXRBJ3DK0AVC3WQ", #mechanical-engineering "../project/Verify-the-axis-model-and-design-discriminating-measurements.lingua#r_2JQWMJQC4W6D8Q0C6KJ47MP30S") { r_3H4Q0D0YXSX1GJHEZ8TB7XCJHP
**Read:** Reuse work/energy, screw-drive and actuator derivations; [FUNdaMENTALS of Design — Alexander Slocum](../sources/FUNdaMENTALS-of-Design-Slocum.lingua#r_58Q6VQJ3FXXGXRBJ3DK0AVC3WQ), topics 6–7. The following original Python program exposes a bounded model, using only the standard library.

A one-direction rest-to-rest move has triangular or trapezoidal speed. Torque is τ=(m+J/r²)ar+Fr, current is I=τ/k_t, copper heating is I²R, and ideal motor voltage is RI+k_tω in consistent SI units. Constant resistance opposes positive travel. Signed shaft work can be negative during deceleration; returning it to a supply depends on hardware outside this model.

```python
from math import isfinite, pi, sqrt

def axis_move(distance, speed_limit, acceleration_limit, mass,
              resistance, lead, rotary_inertia, torque_constant, resistance_ohm):
    values = (distance, speed_limit, acceleration_limit, mass, resistance,
              lead, rotary_inertia, torque_constant, resistance_ohm)
    if not all(isfinite(v) for v in values):
        raise ValueError("finite inputs required")
    if min(distance, speed_limit, acceleration_limit, lead, torque_constant) <= 0:
        raise ValueError("positive motion and conversion inputs required")
    if min(mass, resistance, rotary_inertia, resistance_ohm) < 0:
        raise ValueError("negative physical parameter")
    ratio = lead / (2 * pi)
    peak = min(speed_limit, sqrt(distance) * sqrt(acceleration_limit))
    acceleration_time = peak / acceleration_limit
    if not all(isfinite(v) and v > 0 for v in (ratio, peak, acceleration_time)):
        raise ValueError("unrepresentable motion")
    cruise_distance = max(0.0, distance - peak * acceleration_time)
    cruise_time = cruise_distance / peak
    equivalent_mass = mass + rotary_inertia / ratio / ratio
    phases = []
    x = t = v = 0.0
    for duration, acceleration in ((acceleration_time, acceleration_limit),
                                   (cruise_time, 0.0),
                                   (acceleration_time, -acceleration_limit)):
        if duration == 0:
            continue
        end_v = v + acceleration * duration
        travel = (v + end_v) * duration / 2
        torque = (equivalent_mass * acceleration + resistance) * ratio
        current = torque / torque_constant
        work = torque * travel / ratio
        copper = resistance_ohm * current * current * duration
        voltages = (resistance_ohm * current + torque_constant * v / ratio,
                    resistance_ohm * current + torque_constant * end_v / ratio)
        phases.append(dict(t=t, duration=duration, x=x, v=v,
                           acceleration=acceleration, travel=travel,
                           torque=torque, current=current, work=work,
                           copper=copper, voltages=voltages))
        t += duration
        x += travel
        v = end_v
    derived = (t, x, v, equivalent_mass, cruise_time)
    derived += tuple(z for p in phases for z in
                     (p["torque"], p["current"], p["work"], p["copper"],
                      *p["voltages"]))
    if not all(isfinite(z) for z in derived):
        raise ValueError("unrepresentable result")
    return dict(phases=phases, time=t, distance=x, final_speed=v,
                peak_speed=peak, equivalent_mass=equivalent_mass,
                mechanical_work=sum(p["work"] for p in phases),
                motoring_work=sum(max(0.0, p["work"]) for p in phases),
                returned_work=-sum(min(0.0, p["work"]) for p in phases),
                copper_loss=sum(p["copper"] for p in phases))

example = axis_move(0.1, 0.02, 0.1, 2.0, 5.0, 0.005, 1e-5, 0.1, 1.0)
print({k: v for k, v in example.items() if k != "phases"})
```

The declared example has 0.2 s acceleration, 4.8 s cruise and 0.2 s deceleration: 5.2 s total, with a 240 rpm peak screw speed. Net shaft work is 0.5 J, the resistance work; start/end kinetic energies agree. Torque remains positive during this example's deceleration because the opposing force exceeds the required net deceleration force. Set resistance to zero to expose negative braking work.

The model omits jerk limits, inductance, elastic modes, friction variation, screw efficiency, drive losses and motor heating dynamics. Its instantaneous acceleration changes imply instantaneous current changes; calculated voltage is not a realizable switching or current-control design.

**Work:** Derive each phase independently and check force, power and energy signs. Compare a short triangular move with a long cruising move. Add one justified physical effect and show which earlier result no longer applies. Do not interpret a nominal encoder increment or this energy calculation as measured machine performance.

